# GOLF/2 tool library — the code page, as a table you can look things up in.
# Authored in code-page mnemonic form.  Prepended after lib/prelude.golfj by
# gtools/build, for programs under gtools/ only; never linked into user code.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR.  tools/codepage.py is the tool the whole build leans on:
# encode turns glyph/\mnemonic source into raw code-page bytes, decode turns raw
# bytes back into glyphs or \mnemonics.  gtools/encode.golfj and
# gtools/decode.golfj are those two in GOLF, and this library is the table they
# both read.  A GOLF program cannot carry the code page as source literals — a
# “…“ block cannot contain byte 0x8E, and the encoder is not string-aware — so
# the table is DATA: ../data/codepage.tsv, generated by tools/mktables.py from
# mkblob2.ATOMS and codepage.COMPILER/LIB, checked in, and gated against
# staleness by test/selfcheck.sh.  Read that file's own header for the format;
# in brief, one record per line, five TAB-separated ASCII fields
#
#     byte(2 hex)  mode(ED|E-)  src(A|C|L)  mnemonic  glyph(hex bytes)
#
# and the three fixed-width fields come first, so a record's head is exactly
# seven characters — two hex digits, TAB, two mode characters, TAB, one source
# letter — so the third TAB is at offset 7 and the mnemonic starts at offset 8.
# `t` relies on that: it takes the op byte from offsets 0..1 and the mode from
# offset 4 without scanning, and only the two variable-width fields are found by
# scanning for the TAB.  60 records today.
#
# IN-MEMORY STRUCTURE, and why it is boring on purpose.  One flat array of
# fixed-size records, scanned linearly.  There are 60 records; GOLF has no hash
# table, no sort and no binary search, and building any of them here would be
# more code than the scan it replaces and would have to be re-justified every
# time the table grows by one row.  A scan of 60 six-cell records is ~60 compares
# per input byte — slower than Python's dict, irrelevant against the syscall
# cost of the tools that use it, and it makes every word in this file the same
# five-line loop.  The decode direction is NOT indexed by op byte into a
# 256-entry table either: that would be a real memory allocation to register in
# ../REGISTRY.md §3 to save a scan the decoder performs once per OUTPUT byte.
# If either direction ever shows up in a profile, `x`/`y` are the two words to
# change and nothing else moves.
#
#   record i is at  tbase + 48*i,  six 64-bit cells:
#     +0   op byte                     (0..255)
#     +8   mode:  0 = ED (decodes back) · 1 = E- (encode-only)
#     +16  mnemonic address — a pointer INTO THE BUFFER YOU PASSED to `t`
#     +24  mnemonic length in bytes
#     +32  glyph address — into this library's own glyph pool
#     +40  glyph length in bytes (1..4)
#
# The `src` column is parsed past and dropped: A/C/L says which Python table a
# row came from, which no lookup here depends on.  Mode is kept because it is
# the asymmetry tools/codepage.py documents — a LIB word named by an ASCII
# letter is reachable by glyph and by \mnemonic on INPUT, but that byte decodes
# as the plain letter, because a low byte could equally be a *user* word of the
# same name.  So `v`/`w` (encode) use every row and `x`/`y` (decode) skip the
# E- ones.  Today mode ED holds exactly the rows with byte >= 0x80 and no other,
# which is what makes this match codepage.decode's `b in BYTE2GLYPH and
# b >= 0x80` — but `x`/`y` test the mode column rather than the byte, because
# the mode column is the thing tools/mktables.py actually derives from it.
#
# The mnemonic column is NOT copied: the records point into the caller's buffer,
# so THE BUFFER MUST STAY ALIVE for as long as the table is used (`y` hands back
# a pointer into it).  The glyph column has to be copied, because the file
# stores it hex-encoded and a byte machine wants the bytes; `t` decodes it into
# a small pool it allocates itself.
#
# MEMORY.  Everything is bump-allocated from the prelude's heap by `A`; this
# library claims NO fixed scratch and adds no row to ../REGISTRY.md §3.  All of
# its state lives in one 16-cell block whose address is kept in the variable
# `s` — the one variable this library owns (§2.1 gives it the letters s–z, and
# `s` is spent on the variable, t–z on the words).  A variable is a 4-byte cell
# and this one holds a heap address (>= 0x500000, far below 2^31, and W6/M-MEM
# would move the heap to 0xC00000, still far below), so it cannot truncate.
# Every cell INSIDE the block is a full 64-bit \fetch/\store, which is what lets
# the buffer live anywhere at all.  Load-time cost for the file as it stands:
# 128 bytes of state + 60*48 bytes of records + 60*8 bytes of glyph pool.
#
# Block layout (offsets from ←s), so two words never share a slot:
#     0   table base            48  field cursor            (t)
#     8   record count          56  current record address  (t v w x y)
#    16   glyph-pool bump ptr   64  best match length       (v w)
#    24   buffer end            72  best match byte / query byte (v w x y)
#    32   line cursor (t) / query pointer (v w)
#    40   line end    (t) / query end     (v w)
#    80   `u` address    88  `u` char
#    96   `z` left ptr  104  `z` right ptr  112  `z` remaining count
#   120   table END — tbase + 48*n, the scan limit every lookup loop tests
# `u` is only ever called from `t` and `z` only from `v`/`w`, but they are given
# slots of their own anyway: sharing is how a table library grows a bug.  The
# table end is stored rather than recomputed because it is constant from the
# moment `t` returns, and all four scanning words test it once per record.
#
# SCRATCH AND RE-ENTRANCY.  No word here touches the prelude's s0–s7, so none of
# them needs an X/Y spill frame — X/Y save s0–s7 and would not protect this
# block anyway.  (`A` is called, and `A` is the documented exception that needs
# no frame of its own.)  What that buys: these words are safe inside a map or a
# fold.  What it does not buy: they are not re-entrant against THEMSELVES, and
# they do not need to be — none of them calls user code.
#
# `\gets` is `\get` + `s`, i.e. `←s`: the encoder takes the longest known
# mnemonic prefix of the alpha run after a `\`, exactly as it takes `\sqr` out
# of `\sqrp`.  ../REGISTRY.md's prefix rule guarantees no future mnemonic can be
# `gets`, so this stays unambiguous.  Likewise `\sets` is `→s`.
#
# All internal pointer/index/flag arithmetic uses the raw atoms \radd \rsub
# \rmul \rmin \rlt, never the bare ASCII + - * ⌈ ⌊, which M-VEC made
# polymorphic.  `\or` `\xor` `/` `?` `,` are not hooked and stay bare.  Byte
# 0x5C (swap) is never emitted — decode renders it as a bare `\` which encode
# would re-read as an escape (prelude.golfj:207-213) — so every value that
# outlives an expression goes through the block instead of being juggled.
# `{…}` is a DO-while, so every loop that can have zero iterations is guarded
# with `<n> 0\eq [ {…} ]`.  The three scan loops inside `t` are the exception
# and cannot be otherwise: each steps the cursor before testing it, and each is
# entered only where the cursor is already known to be inside its field.
#
# WORDS.  Call `t` once, before anything else.
#   t  buf len -> n     parse the TSV in [buf,buf+len) into the table; -> records
#   u  a -> b           the byte spelled by the two hex digits at address a
#   v  p e -> b k       LONGEST glyph matching the bytes at p (never past e):
#                       op byte and bytes consumed, or (-1, 0) for no match
#   w  p e -> b k       LONGEST mnemonic that is a prefix of the bytes at p
#                       (never past e): op byte and letters consumed, or (-1, 0)
#   x  b -> p k         byte -> glyph bytes, ED rows only; k = 0 = no glyph
#   y  b -> p k         byte -> mnemonic bytes, ED rows only; k = 0 = none
#   z  p q k -> f       0 iff the k bytes at p equal the k bytes at q
# `v` and `w` are deliberately the same word twice over two different columns —
# longest match wins in both, and the only difference is 32/40 versus 16/24.
# Callers must pass p <= e: the fits-in-the-window test is unsigned, so p > e
# would wrap and let a glyph match past the end.
#
# `w`'s window is what gives the encoder its longest-known-prefix rule for free:
# scan the run of ASCII letters after the `\`, pass its two ends, and \sqr48 is
# \sqr then 48 while \sqrp is \sqr then the word p — the caller writes no loop.
#
# GLYPH MATCHING NEEDS NO UTF-8 DECODING.  Across all 55 non-ASCII glyphs no
# glyph's UTF-8 encoding is a prefix of another's — UTF-8 guarantees it
# structurally, since a continuation byte can never start a sequence — so at
# most one record can match at a given position and `v` may work on the raw byte
# stream.  It still takes the LONGEST match rather than the first, so the
# invariant is a fact about the data and not a thing this code depends on.
# Five glyphs ARE ASCII (~ $ | = >) and map to their own code points; the Python
# encoder consults the glyph table BEFORE the ASCII passthrough and a port must
# keep that order, so call `v` first even though today the two agree.
#
# `u` decodes one hex pair, branchlessly: nib(c) = c-48 + 7*(64 <u c), since
# `\rlt` yields -1, so a letter gets its extra -7 and a digit does not.  The
# generated file only ever writes uppercase A-F.
:u\gets80\radd\store
 \gets80\radd\fetch?\gets88\radd\store \gets88\radd\fetch48\rsub 64\gets88\radd\fetch\rlt7\rmul\radd16\rmul
 \gets80\radd\fetch1\radd?\gets88\radd\store \gets88\radd\fetch48\rsub 64\gets88\radd\fetch\rlt7\rmul\radd\radd;
# `z` compares k bytes without a branch either: it ORs together the XOR of each
# pair, so the result is 0 exactly when every byte agreed — and 0 is true, so
# the caller writes `… z[ hit ]`.  The accumulator rides the data stack under
# the loop flag; only the three cursors need cells.
:z\gets112\radd\store\gets104\radd\store\gets96\radd\store 0
 \gets112\radd\fetch 0\eq[{\gets96\radd\fetch?\gets104\radd\fetch?\xor\or
 \gets96\radd\fetch1\radd\gets96\radd\store \gets104\radd\fetch1\radd\gets104\radd\store
 \gets112\radd\fetch1\rsub\gets112\radd\store \gets112\radd\fetch 0\eq}];
# `t` walks the buffer twice.  Pass A counts the lines that do not start with
# `#`, because the record count decides both allocations and GOLF's allocator
# cannot grow a block.  Pass B fills the next record and appends its glyph bytes
# to the pool.  Both passes share one line-scan idiom: step the cursor, stop when it
# reaches the end OR lands on a newline, and read through \rmin against e-1 so a
# file with no final newline walks off nothing.  Pass A scans with the line-end
# cursor so the line cursor still holds `buf` when pass B starts.
:t 16A\sets \gets24\radd\store \gets32\radd\store
 \gets32\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch\radd\gets24\radd\store 0\gets8\radd\store
 \gets32\radd\fetch\gets40\radd\store
 \gets40\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd[{\gets40\radd\fetch?35\eq[\gets8\radd\fetch1\radd\gets8\radd\store]
  {\gets40\radd\fetch1\radd\gets40\radd\store \gets40\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd
   \gets40\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch1\rsub\rmin?10\eq\or}
  \gets40\radd\fetch1\radd\gets40\radd\store \gets40\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd}]
 \gets8\radd\fetch6\rmul A\gets\store \gets8\radd\fetch A\gets16\radd\store
 \gets\fetch\gets8\radd\fetch48\rmul\radd\gets120\radd\store
 \gets\fetch\gets56\radd\store
 \gets32\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd[{\gets32\radd\fetch\gets40\radd\store
  {\gets40\radd\fetch1\radd\gets40\radd\store \gets40\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd
   \gets40\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch1\rsub\rmin?10\eq\or}
  \gets32\radd\fetch?35\eq[\gets32\radd\fetch u\gets56\radd\fetch\store
   \gets32\radd\fetch4\radd?68\eq1\radd\gets56\radd\fetch8\radd\store
   \gets32\radd\fetch8\radd\gets48\radd\store \gets48\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch16\radd\store
   {\gets48\radd\fetch1\radd\gets48\radd\store \gets48\radd\fetch\gets40\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd
    \gets48\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch1\rsub\rmin?9\eq\or}
   \gets48\radd\fetch\gets32\radd\fetch8\radd\rsub\gets56\radd\fetch24\radd\store
   \gets16\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch32\radd\store
   \gets40\radd\fetch\gets48\radd\fetch1\radd\rsub2/\gets56\radd\fetch40\radd\store
   \gets48\radd\fetch1\radd\gets48\radd\store
   \gets48\radd\fetch\gets40\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd[{\gets48\radd\fetch u\gets16\radd\fetch,
    \gets16\radd\fetch1\radd\gets16\radd\store \gets48\radd\fetch2\radd\gets48\radd\store
    \gets48\radd\fetch\gets40\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd}]
   \gets56\radd\fetch48\radd\gets56\radd\store]
  \gets40\radd\fetch1\radd\gets32\radd\store \gets32\radd\fetch\gets24\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd}]
 \gets8\radd\fetch;
# `v` glyph -> byte.  Three nested guards per record, cheapest first: does the
# glyph still fit in [p,e)?  is it longer than the best so far?  do the bytes
# match?  The record pointer walks by 48 rather than being indexed, and stops
# against the table end `t` parked at +120, so the whole loop is free of
# multiplies.  Result (-1, 0) when nothing matched — k is the marker a caller
# should test, b is only a sentinel.
:v\gets40\radd\store\gets32\radd\store 0\gets64\radd\store 0 1\rsub\gets72\radd\store
 \gets\fetch\gets56\radd\store
 \gets8\radd\fetch 0\eq[{\gets40\radd\fetch\gets32\radd\fetch\rsub\gets56\radd\fetch40\radd\fetch\rlt[
   \gets64\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch40\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd[
    \gets32\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch32\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch40\radd\fetch z[
     \gets56\radd\fetch40\radd\fetch\gets64\radd\store \gets56\radd\fetch\fetch\gets72\radd\store]]]
  \gets56\radd\fetch48\radd\gets56\radd\store
  \gets56\radd\fetch\gets120\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd}]
 \gets72\radd\fetch\gets64\radd\fetch;
# `w` mnemonic -> byte: `v` again over columns 16/24 instead of 32/40.  Pass the
# two ends of the run of ASCII letters and the longest known prefix falls out.
:w\gets40\radd\store\gets32\radd\store 0\gets64\radd\store 0 1\rsub\gets72\radd\store
 \gets\fetch\gets56\radd\store
 \gets8\radd\fetch 0\eq[{\gets40\radd\fetch\gets32\radd\fetch\rsub\gets56\radd\fetch24\radd\fetch\rlt[
   \gets64\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch24\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd[
    \gets32\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch16\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch24\radd\fetch z[
     \gets56\radd\fetch24\radd\fetch\gets64\radd\store \gets56\radd\fetch\fetch\gets72\radd\store]]]
  \gets56\radd\fetch48\radd\gets56\radd\store
  \gets56\radd\fetch\gets120\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd}]
 \gets72\radd\fetch\gets64\radd\fetch;
# `x` byte -> glyph and `y` byte -> mnemonic, for the decoder.  Op bytes are
# unique (codepage.py check enforces it), so the first match is the only one and
# `^` returns straight out of the loop.  The mode guard is what makes 0x52
# encode from ⍳ and \range but decode as the letter R.  Falling off the end
# pushes (0, 0): a byte with no ED row decodes as ASCII, or as \xHH.
:x\gets72\radd\store\gets\fetch\gets56\radd\store
 \gets8\radd\fetch 0\eq[{\gets56\radd\fetch\fetch\gets72\radd\fetch\eq1\radd[
   \gets56\radd\fetch8\radd\fetch[\gets56\radd\fetch32\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch40\radd\fetch^]]
  \gets56\radd\fetch48\radd\gets56\radd\store
  \gets56\radd\fetch\gets120\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd}]
 0 0;
:y\gets72\radd\store\gets\fetch\gets56\radd\store
 \gets8\radd\fetch 0\eq[{\gets56\radd\fetch\fetch\gets72\radd\fetch\eq1\radd[
   \gets56\radd\fetch8\radd\fetch[\gets56\radd\fetch16\radd\fetch\gets56\radd\fetch24\radd\fetch^]]
  \gets56\radd\fetch48\radd\gets56\radd\store
  \gets56\radd\fetch\gets120\radd\fetch\rlt1\radd}]
 0 0;
